+# Editorial review — Movement 6, chapters 35–40
+
+## Review status and verdict
+
+This is an informed editorial review by a separate review seat, not a manuscript-only blind read and not a real human audience test. Coverage is the complete supplied movement, chapters 35–40, in order, with the compiled canon evidence, formula, measured metrics, and supplied overlap report.
+
+**Verdict: close with two line-level fixes, then targeted recheck.** The movement delivers the book's central revelation with unusual patience and moral force. Its calendar, injuries, knowledge boundaries, fragment limits, secret reach, reserved disclosures, and protected wording all hold. No scene-scale or structural repair is warranted. The remaining defects are a contradictory explanation of the clean door's forty-day trigger in chapter 38 and a one-person count error in chapter 40.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+Interest caught immediately in chapter 35's first line, because the new Anchor-adjacent perception has stopped asking and Cael does not trust the silence. It tightened when the case came off the shelf and fully caught at the river-town father's letter: that letter turns institutional harm from a theory into one child asking her father what she is. The strongest emotional material belongs to Seln—especially Lira asking what it cost him to remain pleasant, Cael revealing the Shadow fragment, and Seln realizing, “You kept my file too, then.” Vastin's refusal to join the circle is the right payoff because it preserves both his integrity and his independence.
+
+Interest slipped only during the densest legal explanation in chapter 38 and, briefly, while chapter 39 restated evidence the reader had already absorbed before moving to the new conclusions. I expect the next movement to test whether the group can keep walking the corridor perfectly after learning it was built to contain them. I would continue without hesitation.
+
+## Reader lenses
+
+Scores are editorial judgments, not audience measurements. Confidence is high unless noted.
+
+| Dimension | Fluent thirteen-year-old lens | Adult genre-reader lens | Located reason |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| Opening pull | 8/10 | 8/10 | **Ch35, opening:** “The new thing asked nothing when he woke.” The bodily recovery, named door, and missing demand create an immediate question before explanation. Confidence: high. |
+| Keep reading | 9/10 | 9/10 | **Ch35, the father's letter; ch36, the leaf; ch40, Vastin's three refusals.** Each local discovery pays off and opens a more consequential question. Confidence: high. |
+| Interest / freshness | 8/10 | 9/10 | **Ch36, “That's a form”; ch39, “It isn't broken. It's maintained.”** Bureaucratic forms become the antagonist without turning the people inside the system into cartoons. Confidence: high. |
+| Clarity / flow | 7/10 | 8/10 | Most deductions are staged object-first and then interpreted. **Ch38, “Forty days after the close of the last sitting he failed to attend”** conflicts grammatically with the following day-75 calculation; this is the one place the movement's hinge cannot be understood cleanly on one pass. Confidence: high. |
+| Character attachment | 9/10 | 9/10 | **Ch36, Lira asking what being pleasant cost; ch37, Seln's terms for the seal; ch40, Vastin declining to join anyone.** Agency and restraint reveal character more strongly than declarations of loyalty would. Confidence: high. |
+| Humor / warmth | 8/10 | 8/10 | **Ch35, Lira calling her ankle a horse; ch38, Brom as “the best doorstop on the bluff”; ch37, the tea and the quarter-turned cup.** The humor belongs to these people and never undercuts the harmed children. Confidence: high. |
+| Action / suspense | 7/10 | 8/10 | **Ch38, the east-hall perception exercise; ch40, the wait in the borrowed room.** The movement is deliberately quiet, but the controlled near-fixing at the chalk line and Vastin's silent reading sustain danger. Confidence: medium-high. |
+| Progression payoff | 8/10 | 8/10 | **Ch38, “It only wants what nobody has,” the ten-foot perception limit, and Brom's count of one.** Anchor-adjacent gains understandable perception, anticipation, limit, and cost without violating the no-deployment lock. Confidence: high. |
+| Connection | 9/10 | 9/10 | **Ch35–38:** Lira's right ankle, Brom's left shoulder, Cael's ribs and forearm, Hesk's clock, the sealed Shadow fragment, the Greyvane leaves, the clean door, and Vastin's northern file all carry forward and pay prior plants. Confidence: high. |
+| Read-aloud quality | 8/10 | 8/10 | Speaker attribution remains clear through five-person councils, and repeated physical anchors—the shaded lamp, flat hands, the kettle—orient the listener. The chapter 38 trigger sentence tangles causality, and **ch40, “Five young people and a man at a window”** creates an audible count error. Confidence: high. |
+
+## Continuity, canon, and story audit
+
+### Findings requiring repair
+
+1. **Chapter 38, line 173, clean-door calculation.** Karis says the entry occurs “forty days after the close of the last sitting he failed to attend,” then immediately uses the fourth sitting's close on day 75 to reach day 115. Cael attended that sitting. The supplied canon fixes the count at forty days after the fourth sitting's close if he leaves while the matter is pending (BOOK_MAP §4e #9; Movement 4 ledger). The current grammar instead makes a missed sitting the trigger. This is a localized explanation error, not a flaw in the clean-door mechanism itself.
+
+2. **Chapter 40, line 279, borrowed-room headcount.** Vastin remembers “Five young people and a man at a window.” The hosts were four young people—Cael, Lira, Brom, and Karis—and Seln at the window. The next paragraph correctly enumerates those four plus Seln. This should read “Four young people and a man at a window.”
+
+### Checks passed
+
+- **Calendar and joins:** Chapter 35 opens on day 88; Lira's strapping comes off after six days, Brom's sling comes off the following day, the five-night indexing leads to the Second-day findings, the decision about Vastin falls on Third-day, and the Ostrand meeting falls on Seventh-day. The sequence agrees with the prior ledger.
+- **Physical state:** Lira's injury remains the right ankle; Brom's is the left shoulder; Cael's left forearm and right ribs continue healing; Karis's right fingers remain paler. No injury disappears early or changes side.
+- **Progression and power limits:** Eight confirmed fragments and one uncounted anomaly remain correct. Shadow is still undeployed. The Anchor session is explicitly perception only; the near-fixing stops before a point or line is laid. Its sense reads rests, thresholds, anticipated placement, and roughly ten feet clearly, consistent with Movement 5. No deliberate hold or second unbinding occurs.
+- **Knowledge boundaries:** Seln learns the integration mechanism and the reason Shadow was sealed. Vastin receives the cache but nothing about Cael's mechanism, Seln's fifteen years of empty product, or the anomaly. Ilsev and Havel remain unknowing. The truth reaches exactly six people: Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln, and Vastin.
+- **Reserved disclosures:** The Compact's falsification is discharged on schedule. *Unbound* appears once, in chapter 39, as the controlled second voicing. The prose advances only the permitted distinction between the older reading and the later advisory function; it names no maker, Architect, will, Quieting, or purpose. The Tide anomaly is explicitly not explained by the cache, and no Tide practitioner appears.
+- **Vastin's arc:** He verifies nothing, reports nothing, takes no paper, joins no side, remains inside the Compact, and refuses certification. His later choice to leave the northern girl's file untouched is morally costly rather than falsely triumphant.
+- **Plan adherence:** The case arrives in ordered objects; Karis supplies four findings and a limit; the clean door is found; Anchor practice stays non-deploying; the borrowed office and three refusals land; the final Vastin window pays the planted northern file. The quiet movement still has one embodied learning encounter.
+
+## Protected wording audit
+
+Every BOOK_MAP §8 protected item that falls within source chapters 15–17 is present as required:
+
+| Protected item | Location | Result |
+|---|---|---|
+| Shadow fragment notice previously protected in Book 4 | Ch37, notice block | Exact six-line notice retained, including *adversarial, non-combat*. |
+| “You kept my file too, then.” | Ch37 | Exact. |
+| “Here is the limit…” | Ch39 | Exact, character for character. |
+| “We've explained the middle of the story. Not the beginning. Entered as a limit.” | Ch39 | Exact. |
+| *Not this either. Still open.* | Ch39 | Exact. |
+| “It's not evidence. It's a map.” | Ch39 | Exact. |
+| Borrowed office description—records administrator, third floor, leasehold building, four streets back, nothing of anyone's | Ch40 | All fixed particulars present; the core sentence is repeated exactly. |
+| “I knew the system was imperfect. I didn't know it was designed to be.” | Ch40 | Exact. |
+
+No protected wording should be touched while repairing the two findings above.
+
+## Reader Standard
+
+**Pass.** The movement is suitable for a fluent thirteen-year-old under the EDITION_BRIEF standard. It contains no profanity, obscenity, crude slang, blasphemy, sexual content, or innuendo. The road violence is recalled through injuries and costs without gore. Moral goodness is active and expensive: Seln preserves children at personal risk; the circle protects Ilsev, Havel, and Vastin from knowledge that would only burden them; Vastin refuses to turn a child into a verification instrument; Cael chooses not to look at every student's badge as a wound. Institutional wrong is named clearly, while honest people inside the institution are treated fairly.
+
+The reading level is mechanically easier than the formula reference, but the administrative reasoning in chapters 38–40 still asks close attention. The clean-door trigger needs the line fix above specifically for the thirteen-year-old one-pass standard.
+
+## Source-reuse gate
+
+The supplied `source-overlap.tsv` reports **0 unprotected shared runs of eight or more words** and **14 protected runs**. This passes the new-prose requirement. The protected runs are allowed and correspond to wording the BOOK_MAP requires. No source-reuse finding is raised.
+
+## Numerical formula alignment
+
+Coverage is all six chapters, 32,503 total words: 32,480 prose words and 23 words in system notices. These are the supplied measurements from `editions/monroe-1.3/tools/formula_metrics.py`; they have not been re-estimated.
+
+| Measure | Target / working range | Observed | Editorial reading |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| Sentence mean | 14.6 / 13–15.5 | 13.57 | Inside the accepted working range, though short of the exact target. |
+| Sentence median | 11 | 10.0 | Slightly short. |
+| Population SD | ~26 | 11.3 | Materially narrower than the research target. This does not mean the prose lacks both short and long sentences; it means the full distribution is less extreme. |
+| Sentences ≤5 words | 27.7% / up to ~34% | 27.0% | Essentially on target. |
+| Sentences ≥40 words | 3.3% / 2.5–4.5% | 3.8% | On target and inside the primary working range. |
+| Paragraph mean | 26.8, directional ASR proxy | 38.21 | Longer than the proxy. |
+| Paragraph median | ~18 / up to ~30 | 21.0 | Close to target and inside the accepted range. |
+| Marked scene breaks | ~8.7 per 10k | 27; 8.31 per 10k | Close. |
+| Words per scene | ~950 / 850–1,050 | 984.9 | On target and inside the primary range. |
+| Flesch Reading Ease | 72.3 | 88.5 | Much easier than the exact formula reference. |
+| Flesch–Kincaid grade | 6.8 / 3.5–6 | 4.29 | Below the exact target but inside the accepted working range and compatible with the Reader Standard. |
+
+The three primary repair measures named by the edition's accepted-drift policy all pass: sentence mean, forty-plus share, and words per scene. A broad rhythm rewrite would therefore be disproportionate and could damage the successful dialogue, document ritual, and protected lines. The low sentence spread and high reading ease should be recorded as drift, not chased in this repair.
+
+**Method.** The tool strips the H1 and marked scene-break lines, removes Markdown emphasis, counts fenced system notices as words but excludes them from sentence and readability statistics, and treats each non-empty line as a written paragraph. It splits sentences at terminal punctuation followed by whitespace and an opening quote/capital, or at paragraph end; common abbreviations and ellipses are protected, and em-dash interruptions do not end a sentence. Syllables use a vowel-group heuristic with silent-*e* and *-le* adjustments; proper names are estimated identically and may bias Flesch results. Paragraph and scene comparisons remain directional because the targets came from ASR pauses, not print typography.
+
+**Unmeasured formula items.** The supplied tool does not measure project-specific progression vocabulary, development-beat rates, POV shares, adverbs, reporting verbs, combat lexicon, fillers, or repeated phrases. No precise values are inferred. By manual segmentation, chapters 35–39 and the first four scenes of chapter 40 are Cael POV; chapter 40's final two scenes, beginning “The Archmarshal sat at his desk,” are one purposeful Vastin cutaway. That shape supports the lead-dominant book plan without pretending this movement must independently equal the book-level 87/13 allocation. Manually identified development turns are well distributed among Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln, and Vastin, but no heuristic rate is claimed. Cumulative book-through-chapter-40 metrics were not supplied, so cumulative numerical alignment remains unmeasured.
+
